welcome back many of America's prisons  are not just overcrowded they're also  understaffed and a deadly incident at a  Delaware Correctional Facility last year  is putting a focus on that problem  Jericka Duncan has been investigating  the case on February 1st 2017 inmates at  Vaughn Correctional Center a  maximum-security prison in Delaware took  six correctional officers and a  counselor hostage I just imagined a  bloodbath elisa Profaci a correctional  officer for nearly 30 years was assigned  to help rescue her colleagues when I got  inside the major was out there and I  said what's going on he said it's not  good they've taken charlie building they  have hostages and there's blood  everywhere six hostages were released  during the 18 hour standoff but 47 year  old lieutenant Stephen Floyd a 16 year  veteran did not make it out alive the  day after the incident I did not go back  Profaci is among more than 200  correctional officers and medical staff  at Vaughn who have resigned or retired  since the uprising according to the  Department of Justice nationwide there  is an average of one guard for every  five inmates on the day of the uprising  Profaci says Charlie building were Floyd  was attacked had just four guards for  127 inmates most prisons in this country  are not adequately staffed Lauren Brooke  Ison is with the Brennan Center for  Justice she says understaffing is a  problem in at least 16 other states and  the need she says is often meant through  overtime most correctional guards are  required to work and ments overtime  we're talking 14 16 18 hour shifts  according to the correctional officers  Association of Delaware during any shift  about 40% of staff is working overtime  ELISA Profaci says it's only a matter of  time before another deadly takeover  happens at another prison my prayers are  with them and I fight to fight for them  until we get what we need to be  and where's that that are safer  conditions for CBS this morning Saturday  Jericka Duncan Wilmington Delaware
 

